<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet>
  <title>6: Green's Theorem</title>
  <alert>MTH 401</alert>

  <theorem xml:id="thm-Greens-theorem">
    <statement>
      <p>
        <term>Green's Theorem: </term> For a two-dimensional field <m>\mbf F = (P,Q)</m>
        and a closed curve <m>C</m> that is oriented counterclockwise (positively oriented) and that encloses the region <m>D</m>, the work done by <m>\mbf F</m>
        along <m>C</m> is 
        <md>
          \ds \oint_C \mbf F \cdot ~d\mbf r = \iint_D  \left(\frac{\partial Q}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial P}{\partial y}\right)~dA = \iint_D |\nabla \times \mbf F|~dA
        </md>.        
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>
  
  <exercise workspace="1in">
    <statement>
      <p>Use Green's Theorem to evaluate the line integral <m>\ds \oint_C -y~dx + x~dy</m> where <m>C</m> is the unit circle oriented in the
        counterclockwise direction.</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="0.4in">
    <statement>
      <p>Evaluate the line integral <m>\ds \oint_C y^2~dx + 3xy~dy</m> where <m>C</m>
  is the curve shown below.  You may want to integrate in polar
  coordinates.</p>
      <image width="25%" margins="0% 75%">
        <shortdescription>A closed curve, see description below.</shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-6-curve1">
        <diagram dimensions="(200,200)" margins="0">
          <coordinates bbox="(-2.5,-1.5,2.5,3.5)">
            <grid-axes spacings="((-2,0.5,2),(-1,0.5,3))" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
            <path start="(1,0)" stroke="blue" arrows="1" thickness="4">
              <lineto point="(2,0)"/>
              <arc center="(0,0)" radius="2" range="(0,180)"/>
              <lineto point="(-1,0)"/>
              <arc center="(0,0)" radius="1" range="(180,0)"/>
\            </path>
            <label anchor="(1.5,1.5)" alignment="n" offset="(0,5)">
              <m>C</m>
            </label>
          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
      <description>
        <p>
          A closed curve <m>C</m> that starts at <m>(1,0)</m>, moves in a straight line to <m>(2,0)</m>,
          continues in a half circle centered at the origin with radius <m>2</m> in the upper half plane until the point <m>(-2,0)</m>,
          then goes in a straight line to the point <m>(-1,0)</m>,
          and then traverses the half circle centered at the origin with a radius of 1 located in upper half plane to end back at the point <m>(1,0)</m>.
        </p>
      </description>
  </image>  
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="1in">
    <introduction>
      <p>Suppose that <m>\ds \mbf F= (xy, \arctan y)</m> and 
  <m>C</m> is the simple closed curve shown below.</p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="0.3in">
      <statement>
        <p>Evaluate the line
  integral <m>\owork{C}{F}</m>.</p>
        <image width="25%" margins="0% 75%">
        <shortdescription>A closed curve, see description below.</shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-6-curve2">
        <diagram dimensions="(200,200)" margins="0">
          <coordinates bbox="(-4,-4,4,4)">
            <grid-axes spacings="((-4,1,4),(-4,1,4))" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
            <path start="(3,0)" stroke="blue" arrows="1" thickness="4">
              <arc center="(0,0)" radius="3" range="(0,180)"/>
              <lineto point="(3,0)"/>
\            </path>
            <label anchor="(2,2)" alignment="ne" offset="(0,5)">
              <m>C</m>
            </label>
          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
      <description>
        <p>
          A closed curve <m>C</m> that starts at <m>(3,0)</m>, continues in a half circle centered at the origin with radius <m>3</m> in the upper half plane until the point <m>(-3,0)</m>, 
          then returns in a straight line along the x-axis to the point <m>(3,0)</m>.  
        </p>
      </description>
  </image>  
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Use your result to find the line integral <m>\ds \work{C'}{F}</m> where <m>C'</m>
  is just the upper semi-circle of radius 3 without the straight line along the x-axis.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="0.3in">
    <statement>
      <p>Each of the fields below has a constant curl.  For each, state
  whether the curl is negative, zero, or positive.</p>
      <sidebyside widths="25% 25% 25%">
    <image>
      <shortdescription>A vector field, all vectors point radially away from the origin.</shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-6-curve3">
        <diagram dimensions="(200,200)" margins="5">
          <definition>f(t,y) = (y[0],y[1])</definition>
          <coordinates bbox="[-4,-4,4,4]">
            <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" />
            <slope-field function="f" system="yes" arrows="yes"/>
          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>
    <image>
        <shortdescription>A vector field, all arrows points counter-clockwise in a co-centric circles centered around the origin.</shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-6-curve4">
        <diagram dimensions="(200,200)" margins="5">
          <definition>f(t,y) = (-y[1],y[0])</definition>
          <coordinates bbox="[-4,-4,4,4]">
            <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" />
            <slope-field function="f" system="yes" arrows="yes"/>
          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>
        <image>
        <shortdescription>A vector field, all arrows point point horizontally towards the y-axis.</shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-6-curve5">
        <diagram dimensions="(200,200)" margins="5">
          <definition>f(t,y) = (-y[0],0)</definition>
          <coordinates bbox="[-4,-4,4,4]">
            <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" />
            <slope-field function="f" system="yes" arrows="yes"/>
          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>
  </sidebyside>

    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="1in">
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Let's make some connections.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="0.01in">
      <statement>
        <p>Sketch the vector field <m>\mbf F(x,y) = (y,0)</m> below. (Ignore the curve already drawn for now.)</p>
        <image width="25%" margins="0% 75%">
        <shortdescription>A closed curve on a graph, see description below.</shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-6-curve6">
        <diagram dimensions="(200,200)" margins="0">
          <coordinates bbox="(-4,-4,4,4)">
            <grid-axes  xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no"/>
            <path start="(1,1)" stroke="blue" arrows="1" thickness="4">
              <lineto point="(1,-1)"/>
              <lineto point="(3,-1)"/>
              <lineto point="(3,1)"/>
              <lineto point="(1,1)"/>
\            </path>
            <label anchor="(2,1)" alignment="ne" offset="(0,3)">
              <m>C</m>
            </label>
          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
      <description>
        <p>
          A grid to sketch a vector field on.  Also included is a closed curve <m>C</m> in the shape of a square oriented 
          counterclockwise through the points <m>(1,1), (1,-1), (3,-1), (3,1)</m>.  
        </p>
      </description>
  </image>  
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Judging from your sketch, is the circulation (the work) around the curve 
  positive or negative?  Explain your thinking.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>What about if the curve were shifted vertically?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Compute <m>\nabla\times\mbf F</m> and explain how the curl is connected
  to the response to the previous two questions.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="1in">
    <introduction>
      <p>Consider the field <m>\ds \mbf F(x,y) = \frac12\left(-y,x\right)</m> and suppose that <m>C</m>
  is a closed curve bounding a region <m>D</m>.</p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Explain why <m>\ds \oint_C \mbf F\cdot d\mbf r</m> measures the area of <m>D</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Verify this is the case by evaluating the line integral directly
  where <m>C</m> is the circle of radius <m>R</m> centered at the origin.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Closed curves and a field with zero curl.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>Suppose that <m>C_1</m> and <m>C_2</m> are two paths that begin at the
  same point and end at the same point and that <m>\nabla\times \mbf F = 0</m>
  in the region in between <m>C_1</m> and <m>C_2</m>.  Explain why <m>\ds \int_{C_1}
  \mbf F\cdot d\mbf r = \int_{C_2} \mbf F\cdot d\mbf r</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>If <m>C</m> is a closed curve and <m>\nabla \times \mbf F = 0</m> in the
  region <m>D</m> bounded by <m>C</m>, explain why <m>\ds \oint_C \mbf F \cdot d\mbf r =
  0</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
